    "finalResponse": "Not every new moon causes a total solar eclipse because the Moon's orbit is tilted about 5° relative to Earth's orbit around the Sun, so most new moons occur when the Moon is too far north or south to cast its shadow on Earth. Even when the Moon is near the alignment line (a node), the eclipse is only total if the Moon is close enough to its closest approach to Earth (perigee) to appear large enough to completely cover the Sun; otherwise, the eclipse is annular or partial. The remaining uncertainty lies in the precise timing of future alignments, which depends on the complex interplay of the Moon's orbital nodes and its elliptical path, but this is well-documented in astronomical models without needing new data.",
